Nice — I'll give you a solid, ready-to-run example that does exactly what you asked:

features

record audio in the browser (start / stop),

upload the audio file to your server,

server stores the file,

server notifies the recipient in real-time via WebSockets (Socket.IO),

recipient gets a notification and can play/download the voice message immediately.

This mirrors how WhatsApp/Chat voice messages work (record locally, upload, notify recipient). It’s simple to adapt for user rooms, authentication, or streaming later.

1) Backend — Node.js + Express + Socket.IO + Multer

Create a folder server/ and inside server/index.js:

// server/index.js
const express = require('express');
const http = require('http');
const path = require('path');
const multer = require('multer');
const fs = require('fs');
const cors = require('cors');
const { Server } = require('socket.io');

const app = express();
const server = http.createServer(app);
const io = new Server(server, {
  cors: { origin: '*' } // in production restrict origin
});

app.use(cors());
app.use(express.json());

// Simple storage folder
const UPLOAD_DIR = path.join(__dirname, 'uploads');
if (!fs.existsSync(UPLOAD_DIR)) fs.mkdirSync(UPLOAD_DIR);

// Configure multer
const storage = multer.diskStorage({
  destination: (req, file, cb) => cb(null, UPLOAD_DIR),
  filename: (req, file, cb) => {
    const unique = Date.now() + '-' + Math.random().toString(36).slice(2, 9);
    // preserve extension
    const ext = path.extname(file.originalname) || '.webm';
    cb(null, `${unique}${ext}`);
  }
});
const upload = multer({ storage });

// In-memory map: userId -> socketId
// In production use Redis or persistent mapping + auth
const userSockets = new Map();

io.on('connection', socket => {
  console.log('socket connected', socket.id);

  // Client should emit "register" with their userId after connecting
  socket.on('register', (userId) => {
    userSockets.set(userId, socket.id);
    console.log(`Registered ${userId} => ${socket.id}`);
  });

  socket.on('disconnect', () => {
    // remove entries mapping to this socket
    for (const [uid, sid] of userSockets.entries()) {
      if (sid === socket.id) userSockets.delete(uid);
    }
    console.log('socket disconnected', socket.id);
  });
});

// Upload endpoint
// Expect fields: fromUserId, toUserId
app.post('/upload-voice', upload.single('file'), (req, res) => {
  try {
    const { file } = req;
    if (!file) return res.status(400).json({ success: false, message: 'No file' });

    const { fromUserId, toUserId, text } = req.body;

    // Build URL to file
    const fileUrl = `${req.protocol}://${req.get('host')}/uploads/${file.filename}`;

    // Prepare payload to send via socket
    const payload = {
      id: file.filename,
      from: fromUserId,
      to: toUserId,
      url: fileUrl,
      createdAt: Date.now(),
      text: text || ''
    };

    // Emit to recipient if online
    const recipientSocketId = userSockets.get(toUserId);
    if (recipientSocketId) {
      io.to(recipientSocketId).emit('voice-message', payload);
    }

    // Optionally also emit back to sender's socket so UI updates
    const senderSocketId = userSockets.get(fromUserId);
    if (senderSocketId) {
      io.to(senderSocketId).emit('voice-message-sent', payload);
    }

    return res.json({ success: true, payload });
  } catch (err) {
    console.error(err);
    return res.status(500).json({ success: false, message: 'Server error' });
  }
});

// Serve uploaded files statically
app.use('/uploads', express.static(UPLOAD_DIR));

// Simple health
app.get('/', (req, res) => res.send('Voice server running'));

const PORT = process.env.PORT || 4000;
server.listen(PORT, () => console.log(`Server listening on ${PORT}`));


Install server deps:

cd server
npm init -y
npm i express multer socket.io cors
node index.js


Now your backend accepts POST /upload-voice (multipart/form-data field file) and will notify the recipient via Socket.IO.

2) Frontend — React component to record, upload and receive messages

Install socket.io client in your React app:

npm install socket.io-client


Create VoiceChat.jsx (React functional component):

// src/components/VoiceChat.jsx
import React, { useEffect, useRef, useState } from 'react';
import io from 'socket.io-client';

const SERVER_URL = 'http://localhost:4000'; // change to your deployed server

export default function VoiceChat({ userId, peerId }) {
  // userId = current user; peerId = the person you're sending to
  const [recording, setRecording] = useState(false);
  const [mediaRecorder, setMediaRecorder] = useState(null);
  const [chunks, setChunks] = useState([]);
  const [messages, setMessages] = useState([]);
  const socketRef = useRef(null);

  useEffect(() => {
    // connect socket
    const socket = io(SERVER_URL);
    socketRef.current = socket;

    socket.on('connect', () => {
      console.log('socket connected', socket.id);
      // register ourselves to server so server knows where to emit
      socket.emit('register', userId);
    });

    socket.on('voice-message', payload => {
      // incoming message from peer
      setMessages(prev => [payload, ...prev]);
    });

    socket.on('voice-message-sent', payload => {
      // confirm sent message; add to UI if you prefer
      setMessages(prev => [payload, ...prev]);
    });

    return () => {
      socket.disconnect();
    };
  }, [userId]);

  const startRecording = async () => {
    try {
      const stream = await navigator.mediaDevices.getUserMedia({ audio: true });
      const mr = new MediaRecorder(stream);
      setMediaRecorder(mr);
      setChunks([]);

      mr.ondataavailable = e => {
        if (e.data && e.data.size > 0) {
          setChunks(prev => [...prev, e.data]);
        }
      };

      mr.onstop = async () => {
        const blob = new Blob(chunks, { type: 'audio/webm' }); // webm wide support
        // optionally show in UI first
        const file = new File([blob], `voice-${Date.now()}.webm`, { type: blob.type });
        await uploadFile(file);
      };

      mr.start();
      setRecording(true);
    } catch (err) {
      console.error('Microphone access denied or error', err);
      alert('Microphone access needed to record voice messages.');
    }
  };

  const stopRecording = () => {
    if (mediaRecorder && mediaRecorder.state !== 'inactive') {
      mediaRecorder.stop();
      setRecording(false);
    }
  };

  const uploadFile = async (file) => {
    const form = new FormData();
    form.append('file', file);
    form.append('fromUserId', userId);
    form.append('toUserId', peerId);

    try {
      const res = await fetch(`${SERVER_URL}/upload-voice`, {
        method: 'POST',
        body: form
      });
      const data = await res.json();
      if (!data.success) {
        console.error('Upload failed', data);
        return;
      }
      // server emits socket events to recipient and back to sender
      console.log('Upload success, payload', data.payload);
    } catch (err) {
      console.error('Upload error', err);
    }
  };

  return (
    <div style={{ padding: 16 }}>
      <h3>Voice chat — you: {userId}  peer: {peerId}</h3>

      <div style={{ marginBottom: 8 }}>
        {!recording ? (
          <button onMouseDown={startRecording} onTouchStart={startRecording}>
            🎤 Hold to record (or click)
          </button>
        ) : (
          <button onMouseUp={stopRecording} onTouchEnd={stopRecording}>
            ⏹ Stop & send
          </button>
        )}
        <span style={{ marginLeft: 12 }}>{recording ? 'Recording…' : ''}</span>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h4>Messages</h4>
        <ul style={{ listStyle: 'none', padding: 0 }}>
          {messages.map(msg => (
            <li key={msg.id} style={{ marginBottom: 12, borderBottom: '1px solid #ddd', paddingBottom: 8 }}>
              <div><strong>{msg.from === userId ? 'You' : msg.from}</strong> • {new Date(msg.createdAt).toLocaleString()}</div>
              {msg.text && <div style={{ fontStyle: 'italic' }}>{msg.text}</div>}
              <audio controls src={msg.url} />
              <div>
                <a href={msg.url} download={`voice-${msg.id}.webm`}>Download</a>
              </div>
            </li>
          ))}
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  );
}

Notes on the component

It uses MediaRecorder (supported in modern browsers). The recorded format is usually webm (good browser support). You can convert to mp3 server-side if needed.

The example triggers recording on onMouseDown / onTouchStart and stops on onMouseUp / onTouchEnd — that gives a "press-to-record" UX. You can change to start/stop clicks instead.

When MediaRecorder stops the data is assembled into a Blob and uploaded.

The server saves the file and emits voice-message to the recipient; the client listens and immediately shows the new message with a playable <audio> element.

3) CORS / Deployment / Production considerations

CORS: io and fetch must be allowed from your frontend domain. In production set the cors options properly.

Authentication: The code uses userId passed in the client props. In real app, map socket connections to authenticated users (JWT/session), not raw client-provided userId.

Storage: Server stores files in uploads/. For scale, store in cloud storage (S3, GCS) and save URLs in your DB.

File type/size: Validate file types and max allowed size in multer options.

HTTPS: Use HTTPS in production. Some browsers require secure context for microphone access.

Format conversion: If you need mp3 or better compatibility, convert server-side with ffmpeg.

Realtime streaming: This example sends completed messages. For true streaming (live voice as you are recording), you need WebRTC — that's more complex. For WhatsApp style voice notes, upload-on-stop works perfectly.

4) Quick run steps (local test)

Run server:

# in server/
node index.js


Create React app (or add component to your existing app), import VoiceChat and render:

<VoiceChat userId="alice" peerId="bob" />
// and on the bob side: <VoiceChat userId="bob" peerId="alice" />


Open two browser windows (or two devices) — one with userId='alice' and the other userId='bob'. Click/hold to record in Alice, stop to send — Bob receives instant notification and can play.

5) Extra enhancements you can add

Show recording length and waveform preview.

Display "sending" / "uploaded" / "delivered" / "played" statuses (emit socket events and handle ACKs).

Use rooms/groups instead of direct user-to-user.

Save metadata in a DB (message history).

Allow transcription by calling an AI speech-to-text model to generate captioned text.

Convert webm to mp3 for cross-device compatibility (ffmpeg).

